<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pelusium</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tell el-Farama</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pelusium was a city at the easternmost mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>, near modern Port Said, guarding the route into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> from Sinai and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a>. Invading armies often fought there: the Persian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambyses-ii">Cambyses II</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/psamtik-iii">Psamtik III</a> nearby in 525 BCE, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artaxerxes-iii">Artaxerxes III</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nectanebo-ii">Nectanebo II</a> in 343 BCE. The Roman general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> was murdered on the shore at Pelusium in 48 BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pelusium.jpg" alt="Pelusium" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jeff Dahl; derivative work by MinisterForBadTimes · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lower_Egypt-en.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Pelusium lay at the eastern extreme of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile-delta">Nile Delta</a>, about 30 kilometres south-east of modern Port Said, between the seaboard and the marshes and about two and a half miles from the sea. Its port was choked by sand as early as the first century BCE, and by the third century CE the city was at least four miles from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flax">Flax</a> of very fine quality grew in the neighbouring lands, and the city was known as an early producer of beer, called the Pelusian drink. It was a border fortress on the frontier, protecting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> on the side of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and the sea, and so was directly exposed to any invader. It was often besieged, and several important battles were fought around its walls.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The battle of 525 BCE, fought near Pelusium, transferred the throne of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaohs</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambyses-ii">Cambyses II</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> wrote that the fields were still strewn with bones when he visited. In 373 BCE the Persian satrap Pharnabazus and the Athenian commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iphicrates">Iphicrates</a> withdrew without attacking, because <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nectanebo-i">Nectanebo I</a> had flooded the neighbouring lands and blocked the channels of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>. About 340 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> took the city, whose garrison held 5,000 Greek mercenaries, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nectanebo-ii">Nectanebo II</a> was defeated in a pitched battle. In 333 BCE it opened its gates to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>.</p><p>Antiochus Epiphanes defeated the troops of Ptolemy Philometor under its walls in 173 BCE, and in 55 BCE Mark Antony took it as cavalry commander of the Roman proconsul Gabinius. Pompey was murdered near it in 48 BCE, and Augustus was admitted in 30 BCE. The Plague of Justinian first emerged at Pelusium in 541. In 639 it offered protracted but ineffectual resistance to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amr-ibn-al-as">Amr ibn al-As</a>, and in 1118 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baldwin-i-of-jerusalem">Baldwin I of Jerusalem</a> razed it. Under the later sultans the harbours were neglected. The city, long in decline, almost disappeared from history.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Pelusium became a Roman provincial capital, that of Augustamnica Prima, and a metropolitan see; its bishop Dorotheus took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-council-of-nicaea">First Council of Nicaea</a> in 325. It remains a titular see of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a> and the seat of a metropolitan bishopric of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Eastern Orthodox Church</a>. The first excavations began in 1910 under the French Egyptologist Jean Cledat, and salvage work started in 1991, when the planned Peace Canal threatened the site.</p><p>Egyptian teams explored the Roman theatre and the Byzantine basilica, and a Polish expedition worked in the Great Theater, of the 2nd or 3rd century, from 2003 to 2009. In 2019 an Egyptian mission revealed a Graeco-Roman building of 2,500 square metres, and in 2022 archaeologists found the remains of a temple of Zeus-Kasios.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pelusium” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 525 BCE Cambyses II defeated Psamtik III of the Twenty-sixth Dynasty of Egypt at <b>Pelusium</b> and took Memphis, making Egypt a province of the Achaemenid Empire.</blockquote><a href="/state/achaemenid-egypt">Achaemenid Egypt</a></li><li><blockquote>In 343 BCE Artaxerxes III defeated Nectanebo II at <b>Pelusium</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/thirtieth-dynasty-of-egypt">Thirtieth Dynasty of Egypt</a></li><li><blockquote>After failed attempts in 351 BCE, the Persian king Artaxerxes III invaded Egypt in 343 BCE, defeating Nectanebo II of the Thirtieth Dynasty of Egypt at <b>Pelusium</b> and capturing Memphis.</blockquote><a href="/state/thirty-first-dynasty-of-egypt">Thirty-first Dynasty of Egypt</a></li><li><blockquote>In 525 BCE the Persian king Cambyses II defeated Psamtik III at <b>Pelusium</b> and took Memphis, turning the country into Achaemenid Egypt.</blockquote><a href="/state/twenty-sixth-dynasty-of-egypt">Twenty-sixth Dynasty of Egypt</a></li></ul></section>
